{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"The first tile sweep cuts the fused kernel substantially, especially with a 64-deep reduction tile, but launch/allocation overhead still dominates the smallest cases. I’m also measuring the native BF16 GEMM path without the redundant materializing copy; if the harness accepts the transposed view’s documented values and shape, that can retain vendor-library MFMA efficiency while removing the extra pass."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport torch, triton\nS=128\nx=torch.randn((64,S,5120),device='cuda',dtype=torch.bfloat16)\nw=torch.randn((1024,5120),device='cuda',dtype=torch.bfloat16)\nfor B in (1,2,4,8,16,32,64):\n    z=x[:B]\n    def linear_view():\n        return torch.nn.functional.linear(z,w).view(B,S,8,128).transpose(1,2)\n    def linear_contig():\n        return torch.nn.functional.linear(z,w).view(B,S,8,128).transpose(1,2).contiguous()\n    a=triton.testing.do_bench(linear_view,warmup=10,rep=50)*1000\n    b=triton.testing.do_bench(linear_contig,warmup=10,rep=50)*1000\n    print(B,f'view={a:.1f} us contig={b:.1f} us',flush=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
